## 1. Introduction
Blue-emitting fluorescent powders have drawn considerable attention for a wide variety of applications in biomedicine, lighting, display, and optical communications.[1] For instance,
to metal ions. Metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT) is a crucial photoluminescence (PL) mechanism in blue-light MOFs based on ligand emission. However, there are few reports on MOFs that emit saturated and efficient deep-blue light (with a wavelength of \({<}450\ \mathrm{nm}\) ), because the mismatch of electronic
transition energies between linker and inorganic node results in weak MLCT and emission.
Improving MLCT is a key task to obtaining high blue-light PL quantum yield (PLQY). Toward this goal, some typical approaches have been developed to suppress the non-radiative decay from the excited state by constructing a rigid environment through crystal engineering, or large \(\pi\)-conjugation.[6] For realizing above protocol, lots of MOFs were required to vary linking units via chemical tailoring with the complicated and laborious preparation processes. As an effective and clean means of modifying the crystal and electronic structures, pressure engineering has been utilized to regulate the optoelectronic properties of various types of materials.[7] Recently, the PL characteristics of MOFs under high pressure have also led to preliminary exploration.[3,8b] Some PL-MOFs with a certain topology (such as scu) exhibit remarkable piezochromic behavior due to the breathing contraction of the framework and conformation changes under high pressure.[8c] On the contrary, it remains a formidable challenge to keep the specific fluorescence of these materials unchanged because they are quite sensitive to the applied pressure. Given their fluorescence would usually be affected by the changes in the conformations and packing modes, it further inspired us to speculate that choosing the rigid MOFs to restrict these parameters via pressure might be an effective strategy to obtain bright deep-blue light MOFs.
Herein, we selected \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) (\(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\): benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid) as a paradigm to successfully achieve outstanding PL performance by pressure-treated engineering. After a pressure treatment, \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) obtained a highly brilliant deep-blue emission with a higher PLQY of up to \(75.0\%\) than the initial \(2.8\%\), which could retain over half a year at ambient conditions. This significantly exceeded that of previously reported deep blue MOFs with emission wavelengths \(<450\ \mathrm{nm}\). The deep-blue emission provided a color coordinate of (0.1514, 0.035), approaching the standard color Rec. 2020 (0.131, 0.046) specification. In addition, a phenomenal pressure-induced emission enhancement (PIEE) behavior was monitored below \(5.1\;\mathrm{GPa}\), which was highly associated with the increased degree of planarization upon compression. The experimental and theoretical results proved that the pressure-treated engineering significantly increased the binding energy by the hydrogen bonding cooperativity effect, which effectively locked the planarization configuration. The optimized planarization configuration increased the electronic transition diversity of MLCT and oscillator strength, accounting for the intriguing high-efficiency PL performance in \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\).
## 2. Results and Discussion
2.1. PL Properties of \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) During a Full Compression and Decompression Cycle
At ambient conditions, the carboxylic acid groups of Y(BTC) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) ligands have three different coordination modes, namely unidentate, bidentate, and free carboxylic acid groups, respectively. The center Y atom is tethered by six oxygen atoms from water molecules as well as three oxygen atoms from \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) to form a tricapped trigonal prismatic geometry. Ultimately,
As a proof of protocol for facilitating the MLCT process by pressure treatment, we loaded \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) samples with a culet size of 400 and \(130\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) diameter cavity in a symmetric diamond anvil cell (DAC) to allow reversible compression. Compared with the initial state of the negligible blue PL, we obtained a bright PL of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) after pressure treatment, and such a high PL state was maintained over half a year (Figure 1b). The corresponding PL spectra showed 27-fold fluorescence enhancement after release of pressure compared to those at ambient conditions (Figure 1c). Under this condition, the PLQY of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) increased from \(2.8\%\) to \(75.0\%\) which was the highest value among the deep blue MOFs with emission wavelengths of \(<450\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1d).[12] Similar Commission International de l'Eclairage (CIE) coordinates of (0.1514, 0.035) at released pressure state and (0.1412, 0.0554) at 1 atm indicated that pressure treatment ensured the monochromaticity of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) (Figure 1e). The strong PL emission peak at released pressure state centered at \(\approx409\ \mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to a deep-blue color, and its CIE coordinates closely approached the standard color Rec. 2020 (0.131, 0.046) specification. With increasing pressure, a gradual PL enhancement was observed and reached a maximum value at \(5.1\ \mathrm{GPa}\), followed by progressive weakening (Figure 1f; Figure S3a, Supporting Information). The PL intensity of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) gradually increased during the decompression and finally improved by 27 times compared with the initial state (Figures S3b and S4, Supporting Information). Optical micrographs clearly revealed the trend of PL intensity in \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) under compression and decompression, along with color changes from dark blue to luminous blue (Figure S5, Supporting Information).
Considering that \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) possessed similar PL with \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) at ambient conditions, we speculated whether the PL phenomenon under high pressure was caused by \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\). To this end, we also investigated the PL of the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) with pressure ranging from 1 atm to \(10.0\ \mathrm{GPa}\). \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) showed a weak blue emission of \(443\ \mathrm{nm}\) at ambient conditions. An arresting emission enhancement was observed in the pressure range of 1 atm \(\approx8.2\ \mathrm{GPa}\). The maximum PL intensity at \(8.2\ \mathrm{GPa}\) was
\({\approx}2\) -fold higher than the one at ambient conditions (Figure S6a, Supporting Information). The PL spectra of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) gradually decreased during the decompression process, and the PL was only slightly enhanced \({<}1\) time compared to the initial state when the pressure was completely released (Figure S6b,c, Supporting Information). A series of optical photomicrographs clearly demonstrated the changes in the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) as a function of pressure (Figure S6d, Supporting Information). Accordingly, the introduction of yttrium ion indeed effectively optimized the rigidity of the framework and eventually obtained a stronger fluorescence characteristic than the initial state, suggesting that the above phenomenon of \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) was not caused by pure \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\).
## 2.2. Evolution of Crystal Structure in \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) Under Pressure
To analyze the underlying pressure-dependent structureproperty relationship of \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6},\) we carried out in situ high-pressure angle dispersive synchrotron X-ray diffraction (ADXRD) measurements during a cycle of 1 atm to \(10.2\ \mathrm{GPa}\) (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The overall ADXRD measurements indicated that all diffraction peaks shifted
to higher angles and \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) did not undergo a distinct structural variation during the whole compression up to \(10.2\ \mathrm{GPa}\) . The Rietveld refinements of the ADXRD patterns at ambient conditions agreed well with the experimental data \((R_{\mathrm{wp}}\,=\,0.84\%\) , \(R_{\mathrm{p}}\,=\,0.57\%)\) . We found that the phase eventually returned to the original crystal structure \((C c)\) when the pressure was released to the ambient condition (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Strikingly, the magnified ADXRD local diffraction peaks showed that the diffraction peaks of (11-1), (22-1), and (20-2) planes located at \(5.4^{\circ}\) , \(77^{\circ}\) , and \(10.04^{\circ}\) did not fully recover to the initial state (Figure 2a). To gain further insight into the structural evolution, we investigated the \(d\) spacings of the three planes as a function of pressure. As seen from Figure 2b, the \(d\) spacings of (11-1), (22-1), and (20-2) planes displayed a gradual decrease with increasing pressure. When the pressure was completely released, the \(d\) spacings of these three planes were smaller than the initial state, corresponding to the incomplete recovery of these peaks. The above behavior may be beneficial to the enhancement of the hydrogen bonds. Figure 2c elucidates the compression rate of the cell parameters with pressures. Different lattice axes exhibited different change rates under high pressure, attributable to the anisotropic compression of \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) . The \(c\) -axis was the most compressible compared to the \(a\) -axis and \(b\) -axis, which indicated that
the \(c\) -axis was more sensitive to pressure. It was found that the continuous compression of the \(c\) -axis made the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction between the layers gradually increase, which could be relevant to the quenching of the luminous intensity under high pressure. Furthermore, we utilized the second-order Birch-Murnaghan equation of state to fit the pressure-volume data obtained in the experiment under high pressure (Figure 2d). The continuous volume collapse indicated that no apparent crystal or electronic structural transitions occurred, and the structure remained stable for \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) . In spite of this, compared with the ambient conditions, the \(a\) -, \(b\) -, \(c\) -axes, and volume were all reduced after pressure treatment, further indicating that the enhancement of hydrogen bonds hindered the recovery of the structure (Table S1, Supporting Information).
## 2.3. Emission Mechanism in Y(BTC) \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) After Pressure Treatment
We performed the Rietveld refinements using the General Structure Analysis System (GSAS) software to study the structural variations of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) . We explored the evolution process of the basic unit of the 3D network structure constructed by the Y atom and the connected three benzene rings under high pressure (Figure 3a). Taking the benzene ring of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (I) connected to the Y atom as the base plane, the distances from the centroids of the benzene ring in
the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (II, III) as well as the Y atom to the base plane are calibrated as \(d_{1},\ d_{2}\) , and \(d_{3}\) . The \(d_{1},\ d_{2}\) , and \({\mathrm{d}}_{3}\) gradually decreased with the applied external pressure, which was consistent with the enhanced emission below 5.1 GPa (Figure S9a, Supporting Information). As seen from Figure 3a, the \(d_{1},\ d_{2}\) , and \(d_{3}\) approached 0.738, 0.721, and \(1.475\mathrm{~\AA~}\) after the pressure was completely released, respectively, which were significantly smaller than the distances at ambient conditions (0.741, 0.764, and \(1.515\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ). Besides, the benzene rings on \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (I) and (III) were always parallel, whereas the dihedral angle \((\theta)\) between the benzene rings on \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (II) and (3) exhibited a decrease with increasing pressure (Figure S9b, Supporting Information). It was noted that the dihedral angle \((\theta)\) changed pronouncedly from \(0.21^{\circ}\) to \(0.00^{\circ}\) after an entire pressure cycle of 1 atm to \(10.2\;\mathrm{GPa}\) . These changes suggested that the integral planarization tendency of the Y atom and the connected three benzene rings increased under pressure, and the planarization could be retained after the pressure was completely released. Therefore, we believe that the PL enhancement of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) might originate from enhanced planarization and rigidification, which could further strengthen the degree of conjugation and facilitate charge transport.[6] We next delved into the reasons for the increased degree of planarization after the pressure treatment and found that hydrogen bonds played an important role. Given that the hydrogen bonds in the three \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) connected to the Y atom were equivalent, we selected \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (I) as an example to analyze the effect of hydrogen bonds
on the planarization of the structure (Figure 3b; Figure S10, Supporting Information). H atoms form \(\mathrm{H}_{1}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\mathrm{O}_{1}^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) hydrogen bonds at \({\approx}1.738\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) after the pressure treatment, which was smaller than the \(\mathrm{H}_{1}{-}\mathrm{O}_{1}\) hydrogen bonds at \({\approx}1.744\mathrm{~\AA~}\) under ambient conditions. Meanwhile, the angle of \(\mathrm{O_{1w}}^{\prime}\mathrm{H}_{1}^{\prime}\mathrm{O}_{1}^{\prime}\) was larger compared to the initial state. Both statements proved the enhancement of \(\mathrm{H}_{1}{-}\mathrm{O}_{1}\) hydrogen bonds. We observed that the \(\mathrm{O}_{4}^{\prime}{-}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{\prime}\cdots\mathrm{O}_{1}^{\prime}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{5}^{\prime}\,{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{5}^{\prime}\,{\cdots}\mathrm{O}_{1}^{\prime}\) hydrogen bonds were relatively weak according to the changes in lengths and angles of hydrogen bonds, resulting in \({\mathrm{O}}_{1}^{\prime}\) being subjected to a downward force along the pink arrow (Table S2, Supporting Information). Moreover, the \(\mathrm{H}_{6}^{\prime}-\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\prime}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\prime}-\mathrm{O}_{6}^{\prime}\) , and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}^{\prime}-\mathrm{O}_{6}^{\prime}\) hydrogen bonds also became stronger, indicating \({\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{\prime}\) and \({\mathrm{O}}_{6}^{\prime}\) are subjected to the hydrogen-bond forces along the green and black arrows. These results prompted the benzene ring in \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (I) move downward, leading to an increase in the angle of \(\mathrm{Y}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{5}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{9}^{\prime}\) after pressure treatment. Similarly, the benzene rings on \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (II) and (III) were also subjected to two downward hydrogen bonding forces, which triggered the angles of \(\mathrm{Y}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{3}^{\prime}\,{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{7}^{\prime}\) and \(\mathrm{Y}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{\prime}\,{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{7}^{\prime}\) became smaller and larger, respectively (Table S3, Supporting Information). Noting that their variation values were larger than that of \(\mathrm{Y}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{5}^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{9}^{\prime}\) , representing that the benzene rings on \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (II) and (III) were both close to the benzene ring in \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (I) upon complete release of the pressure.
To further verify the change of intermolecular interactions under high pressure, especially the interactions of hydrogen bonds, we carried out in situ high-pressure IR measurements on \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) (Figure 3c; Figure S11, Supporting Information). The peaks at 763 and \(710~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) could be assigned as out-of-plane deformation vibration of aromatic carbon atoms.[13] Both of the peaks broadened and split upon compression to above \(5.3\ \mathrm{\textrm{GPa}}\) , typically suggesting the enhanced ring-ring interactions, which may be deeply rooted in the decrease of PL after \(5.3\ \mathrm{GPa}\) .[14] We further conducted the IR measurements of \(\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) during decompression process (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The experimental results showed that the peaks at 763 and \(710~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) gradually strengthened during decompression. Notably, the peaks located at \(763~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) gradually merged into a single peak during decompression. These results indicated the weakening of the ring-ring interactions. Namely, the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction during decompression became weak, resulting in a gradual increase in PL intensity during decompression.[14] The characteristic bands in the regions of 1614–1559 and \(1437{-}1373~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were assigned to the symmetric and antisymmetric stretching vibration of carboxylate groups, respectively (Figure S11, Supporting Information).[15] These bands proved that the \(\mathrm{Y}^{3+}\) ions have been successfully coordinated with the \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) ligands.[16] With increasing applied pressure, the characteristic bands in the regions of 1614–1559 and
1437–1373 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) continuously shifted to a higher wavenumber, which originated from the reduction of the interatomic distance. The bands in the spectral region of \(3600{-}2800~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were for the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching vibrations \(\nu(\mathrm{O-H})\), showing an evidently red shift by the range of \(0{-}6.2\ \mathrm{GPa}\). It was intriguing that the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching vibration mode at \(3341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) displayed a redshift of \(9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) after the pressure treatment. This corresponding redshift proved that the \(\mathrm{O-H\cdots O}\) hydrogen bonds were strengthened. With the aim of quantifying the hydrogen bond strength, the hydrogen bond binding energies were calculated after pressure treatment (Figure 3d). The total energy of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) structure after pressure treatment was \(0.31\,\mathrm{eV}\) higher than the initial state, indicating that the pressure-treated structure is in a metastable state. Furthermore, the hydrogen bond binding energy was \(0.96~\mathrm{eV}\) upon complete release of the pressure. This result indicated that the pressure-treated structure required to overcome \(0.96~\mathrm{eV}\) energy to recover to the initial state. Namely, the structure is in a high energy state that is difficult to recover after complete release of the pressure, which is caused by the enhanced hydrogen bonds. Thus, the planarization structure after pressure treatment could be retained. With the above considerations, the strengthening of the hydrogen
bonds after pressure was released successfully locked its planarization structure.
2.4. Calculated Energy Diagram Associated MLCT in Y(BTC) \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}\) Before and After Pressure Treatment
To further shed light on the mechanism of the PL, we examined the electronic structure in the \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) by the density functional theory (DFT) and time-dependent DFT (TDDFT) methods based on B3LYP/6-31G (d, p) level basis set and quantitatively analyzed the PL origin. Figure 4a presents the optimized molecular geometry and the spatial distributions of the molecular orbitals. At ambient conditions, the electron-density distribution of highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) was located on the carboxylic acid groups, whereas the electron-density distribution of lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) and LUMO+1 was governed by metal yttrium ions component. At this point, the MLCT transition of d \((\mathrm{Y})\rightarrow\mathrm{p}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})\) was the predominant charge transfer mode (accounting for \(89\%\)). The excitations after complete release of the pressure were dominated by the LUMO \(\rightarrow\) HOMO-1 and
LUMO+1 → HOMO transitions. Electron-density distribution of HOMO-1 was located on the carboxylic acid group and benzene ring of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\), whereas electron-density distribution of HOMO was situated on carboxylic acid group. Electronic transition at this time was mainly assigned to the MLCT of \(d_{z}\) (Y) \(\rightarrow\mathrm{p}\) \((\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})\) and \(\pi\) \((\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})\) and accompanied by the MLCT of \(d_{\mathrm{xy}}\) (Y) \(\rightarrow\mathrm{p}\) \((\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})\), with the proportions of transition process of \(57\%\) and \(35\%\) respectively. Thus, pressure treatment increased the transition diversity of MLCT, caused by enhanced planarization. Noted that the united oscillator strength of the two transition routes was 1.1081, which was higher than that of the initial single transition route (0.9030). The superposition effect resulting from pressure treatment would make the system exhibit a higher-intensity transition peak. Therefore, the improved PL intensity could be mainly ascribed to the transition diversity of MLCT caused by the enhanced conjugation and planarization along with the subsequent increased oscillator strength (Figure 4b). The calculated electronic transition energy of \(2.88~\mathrm{eV}\) after pressure treatment was comparable to that of the ambient conditions \((2.82\,\mathrm{\eV})\), ensuring the blue monochromaticity of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\). This further proved that we have successfully realized a bright deep blue light enhancement by means of pressure treatment.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a pressure-treated strategy was successfully developed to achieve significant PL enhancement of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\). The PIEEE phenomenon was achieved at a mild pressure of 5.1 GPa. The enhanced PL emission was attributed to the effect of planarization and rigidification. More importantly, Y(BTC) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\) exhibited an excellent PLQY of up to \(75.0\%\) through pressure treatment compared with the initial value of \(2.8\%\). In light of both experimental and calculation results, the hydrogen bonds enhanced by the hydrogen bonding cooperativity effect successfully locked the planarization structure. Therefore, this promoted the diversity of MLCT and increased the oscillator strength, resulting in the ultimate PL enhancement of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\). Accordingly, we successfully obtained a bright deep blue light using high pressure, which is of great significance in solid-state lighting. Pressure-treated engineering could effectively optimize the optical properties of carboxylate-based MOFs materials with planarization conformation and hydrogen bond interactions, and also provide a promising strategy for the development of lighting, sensing, display, and potentially many others.
